[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: StrictDoc mints a fresh MID on every parse, so two reads of one file disagree

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-MID-IS-PER-PARSE-RANDOM
TITLE: StrictDoc mints a fresh MID on every parse, so two reads of one file disagree
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3 while writing extract/compare.py. Every node StrictDoc
builds carries a `mid`, and for a .sgra it is generated fresh on each parse --
a .sgra has no syntax to declare one. Reading the SAME BYTES twice therefore
produces two models that disagree on every `mid` in the file.

Any structural comparison of two models has to skip it. Keeping it is not a
stricter comparison, it is one that can never pass, and because the difference
is per-parse rather than per-file it does not look like a bug in whatever
produced the second file.

The consequence beyond comparison: a MID is not an identity that survives a
re-read, so nothing may key on one across parses.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The first run of compare.py against two byte-identical files reported them
different, with a mid mismatch on every node. Worth knowing before anything
else -- a diff tool, a cache key, a cross-file reference -- treats a MID as
stable.
<<<
